{"units":[{"id":"u1","kind":"warning","title":"Peeking inflates false positives","claim":{"text":"The source warns that testing after every observation and stopping at first p<0.05 turns a nominal 5% false positive rate into 26.1%.","quote":"Miller's worst case: testing after every observation and stopping at first p<0.05 turns a nominal 5% false positive rate into 26.1%."},"action":null,"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Testing after every observation and stopping at first p<0.05.","quote":"testing after every observation and stopping at first p<0.05"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No direct recommendation on whether to avoid peeking or use sequential methods is stated in this unit; sequential methods are covered separately."],"relations":[{"kind":"alternative_to","target":"u2"}]},{"id":"u2","kind":"principle","title":"Sequential methods legitimise looking early","claim":{"text":"The source states that sequential methods legitimise looking early, listing mSPRT, GAVI, group sequential design with alpha spending, and corrected-alpha approach.","quote":"Sequential methods legitimise looking early:"},"action":null,"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["Tradeoff: always-valid methods cost power (wider intervals) versus a correctly-sized fixed test; Spotify's guidance is conditional on ability to estimate expected sample size."],"relations":[{"kind":"alternative_to","target":"u1"}]},{"id":"u3","kind":"action","title":"Run a minimum test duration of at least one week","claim":{"text":"The source recommends a minimum runtime of at least one full week, and in practice two, to cover the weekly business cycle and returning visitors.","quote":"run at least one full week, and in practice two"},"action":{"text":"Run at least one full week, and in practice two, to cover the weekly business cycle.","quote":"run at least one full week, and in practice two"},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No specific stopping rule or metric for determining the full cycle beyond the statement is given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u4","kind":"action","title":"Diagnose novelty and primacy effects","claim":{"text":"The source describes novelty and primacy as threats to external validity, and says to diagnose by plotting daily effect over time and comparing first-exposure vs repeat-exposure conversion for the variant.","quote":"Diagnose by plotting daily effect over time and by comparing first-exposure vs repeat-exposure conversion for the variant."},"action":{"text":"Diagnose novelty/primacy by plotting daily effect over time and comparing first-exposure vs repeat-exposure conversion.","quote":"Diagnose by plotting daily effect over time and by comparing first-exposure vs repeat-exposure conversion for the variant."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Novelty or primacy effect is suspected.","quote":"Novelty and primacy effects."}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No threshold for how much daily effect change indicates novelty/primacy is stated.","Kohavi's finding that most suspected effects are statistical artifacts of early noisy cumulative deltas is noted but no test is specified."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u5","kind":"action","title":"Use bold big-swing tests for low-traffic sites","claim":{"text":"The source's governing principle for below-threshold traffic is to shift from statistical proof to learning fast and cheaply; it recommends bold, big-swing A/B tests with differentiated changes targeting 10-20%+ effect rather than tweaks below about 100,000 visitors/month.","quote":"The governing principle: below the A/B threshold, the job shifts from proving things statistically to learning fast and cheaply. Test bigger, not more.

Methods and when each applies:

Bold, big-swing A/B tests. Below about 100,000 visitors/month, default to differentiated changes with a target effect of 10-20%+, not tweaks"},"action":{"text":"Default to differentiated changes with a target effect of 10-20%+, not tweaks, below about 100,000 visitors/month.","quote":"Below about 100,000 visitors/month, default to differentiated changes with a target effect of 10-20%+, not tweaks"},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Site has below about 100,000 visitors/month.","quote":"Below about 100,000 visitors/month"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[{"text":"Avoid tweaks.","quote":"not tweaks"}]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["The source says one bold homepage test teaches more than 10 micro-tests on low-traffic category pages, but no metric for 'teaches more' is defined."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u6","kind":"action","title":"Concentrate tests on higher-traffic pages","claim":{"text":"The source recommends concentrating tests where the traffic is: homepage, top product pages, cart, and not testing low-traffic category pages.","quote":"Concentrate tests where the traffic is: homepage, top product pages, cart. Do not test low-traffic category pages."},"action":{"text":"Concentrate tests where the traffic is: homepage, top product pages, cart. Do not test low-traffic category pages.","quote":"Concentrate tests where the traffic is: homepage, top product pages, cart. Do not test low-traffic category pages."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[{"text":"Do not test low-traffic category pages.","quote":"Do not test low-traffic category pages."}]},"completion":[],"unknowns":[],"relations":[]},{"id":"u7","kind":"action","title":"Use sequential before/after with honest error bars only under strict conditions","claim":{"text":"The source describes sequential before/after with honest error bars as a method only when parallel testing is impossible and external conditions can be held constant; it is best for large structural changes and should be treated as weak evidence.","quote":"Sequential before/after with honest error bars. Only when parallel testing is impossible and you can hold external conditions constant. Best for large structural changes. Treat as weak evidence."},"action":{"text":"Use sequential before/after with honest error bars only when parallel testing is impossible and external conditions can be held constant.","quote":"Sequential before/after with honest error bars. Only when parallel testing is impossible and you can hold external conditions constant."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Parallel testing is impossible and external conditions can be held constant.","quote":"Only when parallel testing is impossible and you can hold external conditions constant."}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Parallel testing is impossible and external conditions can be held constant.","quote":"Only when parallel testing is impossible and you can hold external conditions constant."}]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No definition of 'honest error bars' is given.","No specific external conditions are listed."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u8","kind":"action","title":"Re-test surprising large winners with a holdout","claim":{"text":"The source recommends re-testing any surprising large winner with a holdout before banking the revenue.","quote":"Re-test any surprising large winner with a holdout before banking the revenue."},"action":{"text":"Re-test any surprising large winner with a holdout before banking the revenue.","quote":"Re-test any surprising large winner with a holdout before banking the revenue."},"trigger":{"op":"any","args":[{"text":"A surprising large winner is observed.","quote":"any surprising large winner"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No definition of 'surprising large' is given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u9","kind":"action","title":"Use qualitative research first when tests are limited","claim":{"text":"The source recommends qualitative research first, using session replay, surveys, and user testing to generate strong hypotheses when you cannot run many tests.","quote":"Qualitative research first. Session replay, surveys, user testing to generate strong hypotheses when you cannot run many tests."},"action":{"text":"Use qualitative research first, with session replay, surveys, and user testing, when you cannot run many tests.","quote":"Qualitative research first. Session replay, surveys, user testing to generate strong hypotheses when you cannot run many tests."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"You cannot run many tests.","quote":"when you cannot run many tests"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No sample size or method selection criteria for qualitative research are given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u10","kind":"action","title":"Use micro-conversions as primary metric with purchase/revenue guardrails","claim":{"text":"The source suggests micro-conversions such as add-to-cart and begin-checkout as the primary metric because they are more frequent and power faster, but warns a lift may not carry through to purchase or revenue; use them as a leading indicator and keep purchase/revenue as guardrails.","quote":"Micro-conversions (add-to-cart, begin-checkout) as primary metric. They are more frequent so they power faster, but the risk is real: a lift in add-to-cart may not carry through to purchase or revenue. Use them as a leading indicator, keep purchase/revenue as guardrails."},"action":{"text":"Use micro-conversions (add-to-cart, begin-checkout) as the primary metric and keep purchase/revenue as guardrails.","quote":"Use them as a leading indicator, keep purchase/revenue as guardrails."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No threshold for when micro-conversion lift is likely to carry through to purchase is given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u11","kind":"action","title":"Use A/B/n and bandits only at 3,000+ orders/month","claim":{"text":"The source says A/B/n and bandits should be used only at 3,000+ orders/month.","quote":"A/B/n and bandits: only at 3,000+ orders/month."},"action":{"text":"Use A/B/n and bandits only at 3,000+ orders/month.","quote":"A/B/n and bandits: only at 3,000+ orders/month."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"At least 3,000 orders/month.","quote":"3,000+ orders/month"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"At least 3,000 orders/month.","quote":"3,000+ orders/month"}]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No further detail on bandit implementation or comparison is given in the supplied text."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u12","kind":"warning","title":"Bayesian methods are not immune to peeking","claim":{"text":"The source flags that Bayesian is not immune to peeking; Alex Molas simulated peeking every 100 observations and stopping when P(A>B)>0.95, pushing false positive rate to about 80%, because a fixed posterior threshold does not control frequentist error under optional stopping.","quote":"Bayesian is not immune to peeking. Alex Molas (October 30 2025) simulated this: peeking after every 100 observations and stopping when P(A>B)>0.95 pushed the false positive rate to about 80%. A fixed posterior threshold does not control frequentist error under optional stopping."},"action":null,"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Peeking after every 100 observations and stopping when P(A>B)>0.95.","quote":"peeking after every 100 observations and stopping when P(A>B)>0.95"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["The simulation details beyond the stated threshold and observation interval are not given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u13","kind":"action","title":"Detect and act on sample ratio mismatch","claim":{"text":"The source defines sample ratio mismatch (SRM) as observed traffic split differing from designed split, recommends detecting with a chi-square goodness-of-fit test on the randomisation unit (users, not page views), and says if SRM is present the result is biased and should not be trusted until the cause is found.","quote":"Detect with a chi-square goodness-of-fit test on the randomisation unit (users, not page views). Kohavi: about 6% of Microsoft experiments show SRM. If SRM is present the result is biased and should not be trusted until the cause is found."},"action":{"text":"Detect SRM with a chi-square goodness-of-fit test on the randomisation unit (users, not page views).","quote":"Detect with a chi-square goodness-of-fit test on the randomisation unit (users, not page views)."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Observed traffic split differs from designed split.","quote":"the observed traffic split differs from the designed split"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["No repair procedure beyond finding the cause is given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u14","kind":"action","title":"Mitigate flicker in client-side tests","claim":{"text":"The source describes flicker (FOOC) in client-side tests where the original page renders briefly before JavaScript swaps in the variant; GrowthBook says it can lead to inaccurate or unreliable results and may even inflate effects, so flicker both hurts UX and biases the read.","quote":"Flicker (FOOC, Flash of Original Content): in client-side tests the original page renders briefly before JavaScript swaps in the variant."},"action":{"text":"Mitigate with anti-flicker snippets (hide body until variant ready), synchronous script loading high in the head, or server-side testing.","quote":"Mitigate with anti-flicker snippets (hide body until variant ready), synchronous script loading high in the head, or server-side testing."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Client-side test renders original page briefly before JavaScript swaps in the variant.","quote":"in client-side tests the original page renders briefly before JavaScript swaps in the variant"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["Anti-flicker snippets hide the page for up to 3-4 seconds by default and slow initial load; no threshold for acceptable cost is given."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u15","kind":"action","title":"Consider CUPED only with appropriate pre-experiment data","claim":{"text":"The source says CUPED uses pre-experiment data as a covariate to strip out variance not caused by treatment; the adjusted estimator stays unbiased with lower variance, and Optimizely's glossary says it can cut sample requirements 30-50% for metrics with strong correlation to historical behaviour. DTC practicality is mixed.","quote":"Uses pre-experiment data (usually the same metric measured before the test) as a covariate to strip out variance not caused by the treatment."},"action":{"text":"Turn on CUPED if your platform offers it and you have returning identified users; it is not a magic fix for a brand with mostly new, anonymous traffic.","quote":"worth turning on if your platform offers it and you have returning identified users; not a magic fix for a brand with mostly new, anonymous traffic."},"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"Platform offers CUPED and you have returning identified users.","quote":"if your platform offers it and you have returning identified users"}]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[{"text":"CUPED needs pre-experiment data keyed to the same user, which means logged-in users or stable device IDs.","quote":"CUPED needs pre-experiment data keyed to the same user, which means logged-in users or stable device IDs."}]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[{"text":"For a DTC brand where most checkout traffic is new or not logged in, the pre-period covariate is often missing or weakly correlated.","quote":"For a DTC brand where most checkout traffic is new or not logged in, the pre-period covariate is often missing or weakly correlated, so the variance reduction is smaller."}]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["The source says simpler cousins help ecommerce more: pick a proportion metric instead of a raw revenue mean, and winsorize outlier orders, but does not give thresholds."],"relations":[]},{"id":"u16","kind":"unresolved_claim","title":"Multi-armed bandits vs A/B tests comparison is cut off","claim":{"text":"The source introduces section 8 on multi-armed bandits vs A/B tests and describes A/B tests, but the supplied text ends after the A/B description; the bandit comparison and continuation are missing.","quote":"A/B test: two periods, explore (split evenly) then exploit (ship winner). Best when you need a clean, high-confidence causal read before a lasting change: redesigns, pricing, navigation, checkout. Gives a defensible answer and a measured effect size."},"action":null,"trigger":{"op":"all","args":[]},"prerequisites":{"op":"all","args":[]},"exclusions":{"op":"any","args":[]},"completion":[],"unknowns":["The section title is '8. multi-armed bandits vs A/B tests', but no bandit-specific guidance or comparison is present in the supplied text.","The continuation after 'Gives a defensible answer and a measured effect size.' is missing."],"relations":[]}],"more_units":true,"unresolved":["Omitted from this batch due to cap: Bayesian vs frequentist pros/cons, how tools report statistical methods (VWO, Optimizely, AB Tasty, Eppo, Statsig, GrowthBook), commercial Bayesian tools using uninformative/flat priors, bot traffic filtering, tracking breakage invalidation, seasonality/promotion overlap, multiple comparisons correction, segment fishing pre-registration, Twyman's law auditing rule, and bandit-specific guidance after the A/B test description.","The multi-armed 